    "summary": "Acoustic‑rich early‑70 set with standout Pigpen country numbers",
    "blurb": "The concert pivots to a rare acoustic second half after Phil’s amp blows, delivering memorable Pigpen‑fronted country and bluegrass tunes. Highlights include Jerry’s lead on “Little Sadie,” Bob’s vocal work on “Long Black Limousine,” and tight rhythm section throughout.",
    "review": "On January 31 1970 the Dead took a detour at The Warehouse when Phil Lesh’s bass amp died after the first set, forcing an impromptu acoustic second half that has become one of the most charming early‑70 shows. The band turned the mishap into a showcase for Ron “Pigpen” McKernan’s organ and vocal grit, slipping into country‑blues territory with songs like “Long Black Limousine,” “Seasons Of My Heart,” and “Saw Mill.” Jerry Garcia shines on the bluegrass‑flavored “Little Sadie” and brings extra bite to the R&B romp “Hard To Handle,” while Bob Weir’s soulful leads anchor the acoustic numbers. The soundboard capture (gd1970-01-31.sbd.cotsman.fixed.24028.sbeok.shnf) is praised as “TOPS,” delivering crystal‑clear banter and ambience with no drop‑outs, making this quirky night a must‑listen for fans of the band’s early acoustic explorations.",
